From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TinyYana <yanasakuranight@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 15:12:06 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Lophinya: complete Folia's synchronization of
 PaperPermissionManager

Folia wrapped every method of that class in synchronized (this) except two:
getDefaultPermissions(boolean), which copies defaultPerms().get(op), and
removePermission(String), which mutates permissions().

The reader is the one that bites. ImmutableSet.copyOf sizes an array from the
set and then fills it, so a concurrent add from another thread's addPermission
aborts the copy with ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException out of
LinkedHashMap.keysToArray. Two threads really do register permissions during
boot: Luminol loads its config on a ForkJoinPool thread and registers Leaves
command permissions from there while the main thread does the same. Measured on
the 38-jar mirror: 3 aborted starts in about 12.

Only a set copy runs under the monitor - no plugin code, no callback, no second
lock - so no lock-ordering edge is added, and the writers keep calling
dirtyPermissibles outside their own block exactly as before.

removePermission is fixed alongside it with no crash of its own on record: a
class synchronized everywhere except one writer is not synchronized.

Kill switch: -Dlophinya.compat.permissionLocking=false

diff --git a/src/main/java/fun/bm/lophinya/compat/LophinyaPermissionLocking.java b/src/main/java/fun/bm/lophinya/compat/LophinyaPermissionLocking.java
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..b21ca2fa866f8204d85b298038ee0692b023280f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/main/java/fun/bm/lophinya/compat/LophinyaPermissionLocking.java
@@ -0,0 +1,41 @@
+package fun.bm.lophinya.compat;
+
+/**
+ * Lophinya: completes Folia's synchronization of {@code PaperPermissionManager}.
+ *
+ * <h2>The gap</h2>
+ * Folia wrapped that class's methods in {@code synchronized (this)} - all of them except two:
+ * {@code getDefaultPermissions(boolean)}, which copies {@code defaultPerms().get(op)}, and
+ * {@code removePermission(String)}, which mutates {@code permissions()}. Everything those two touch
+ * is guarded everywhere else.
+ *
+ * <p>The reader is the one that actually bites. {@code ImmutableSet.copyOf} allocates an array from
+ * the set's current size and then fills it; if another thread adds a permission in between,
+ * {@code LinkedHashMap.keysToArray} runs off the end and throws
+ * {@code ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException}. Two threads really do register permissions concurrently
+ * during boot: Luminol loads its config on a {@code ForkJoinPool} thread and registers Leaves
+ * command permissions from there ({@code ConfigsInstance.setupLatch} ->
+ * {@code RootNode.register} -> {@code CommandUtils.registerPermissions}) while the main thread is
+ * doing the same. Measured on the 38-jar mirror: <b>3 aborted starts in about 12</b>.
+ *
+ * <p>This is a defect in the execution base, not a plugin compatibility gap - it happens with no
+ * plugins loaded at all, and it aborts the whole server rather than one plugin.
+ *
+ * <h2>Why locking the reader is enough, and safe</h2>
+ * The writers already hold this monitor while they mutate. Making the copy hold it too closes the
+ * window. Only {@code Set.toArray} runs inside the block - no plugin code, no callback, no second
+ * lock - so no lock-ordering edge is added. That distinction is exactly why the writers push
+ * {@code dirtyPermissibles} (which does call into arbitrary {@code Permissible} implementations)
+ * outside their own block, and this change keeps that arrangement untouched.
+ *
+ * <p>Kill switch: {@code -Dlophinya.compat.permissionLocking=false} restores the unsynchronized
+ * upstream bodies, and with it the boot crash.
+ */
+public final class LophinyaPermissionLocking {
+
+    public static final boolean ENABLED =
+        !"false".equalsIgnoreCase(System.getProperty("lophinya.compat.permissionLocking", "true"));
+
+    private LophinyaPermissionLocking() {
+    }
+}
diff --git a/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/PaperPermissionManager.java b/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/PaperPermissionManager.java
index 9ddbb2d72e11c6abbbdb866f3010f276efceda41..430fe91af585a7dc3a226270e59b17aa24b50776 100644
--- a/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/PaperPermissionManager.java
+++ b/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/PaperPermissionManager.java
@@ -77,7 +77,21 @@ abstract class PaperPermissionManager implements PermissionManager {
     @Override
     @NotNull
     public Set<Permission> getDefaultPermissions(boolean op) {
+        // Lophinya start - Folia synchronized the writers of defaultPerms() but not this reader.
+        // ImmutableSet.copyOf sizes the array first and then fills it, so a concurrent add from
+        // another thread's addPermission/recalculatePermissionDefaults aborts the copy with
+        // ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException. That is not hypothetical: Luminol loads its config on a
+        // ForkJoin thread and registers Leaves command permissions from there while the main thread
+        // is still registering its own, and the server dies during boot roughly one start in four.
+        // Nothing but a set copy runs under the monitor, so this adds no lock-ordering edge - which
+        // is why the writers deliberately call dirtyPermissibles outside their own block.
+        if (!fun.bm.lophinya.compat.LophinyaPermissionLocking.ENABLED) {
+            return ImmutableSet.copyOf(this.defaultPerms().get(op)); // stock body, kill switch
+        }
+        synchronized (this) {
         return ImmutableSet.copyOf(this.defaultPerms().get(op));
+        }
+        // Lophinya end
     }
 
 
@@ -89,7 +103,18 @@ abstract class PaperPermissionManager implements PermissionManager {
 
     @Override
     public void removePermission(@NotNull String name) {
+        // Lophinya start - the second method Folia's synchronization sweep missed. Every other
+        // mutation of permissions() holds the monitor; this one races getPermissions()'s copy the
+        // same way getDefaultPermissions raced above. No crash observed from it yet - it is fixed
+        // because a class that is synchronized everywhere except one writer is not synchronized.
+        if (!fun.bm.lophinya.compat.LophinyaPermissionLocking.ENABLED) {
+            this.permissions().remove(name.toLowerCase(java.util.Locale.ENGLISH)); // stock body, kill switch
+            return;
+        }
+        synchronized (this) {
         this.permissions().remove(name.toLowerCase(java.util.Locale.ENGLISH));
+        }
+        // Lophinya end
     }
 
     @Override
